1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,396
Chapter 2. The coin box.

2
00:00:03,696 --> 00:00:12,450
Ralph Baer was an engineer with a question: if almost every home had a television… why was it only used for watching?

3
00:00:12,630 --> 00:00:22,970
By 1968 he had a prototype. In 1972 it went on sale as the Magnavox Odyssey: the first home console.

4
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:32,358
One of its games was ping-pong. A young man named Nolan Bushnell tried it at a demonstration. Weeks later, he founded Atari.

5
00:00:32,538 --> 00:00:40,840
His engineer, Al Alcorn, built his own version inside a wooden cabinet. They left it in a California bar, just to see what would happen.

6
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:45,020
A few days later they got a call: the machine wasn't working.

7
00:00:45,570 --> 00:00:50,400
Alcorn opened it up. It wasn't broken. It was overflowing with coins.

8
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:52,484
It was called Pong.
